WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.165 --> 00:00:04.371
Today's video has been made possible by the Forgotten City.

00:00:04.371 --> 00:00:14.433
If an open world mystery adventure game based in a cursed ancient Roman city sounds interesting then stick around until the end to find out more.

00:00:59.949 --> 00:01:04.672
Pūblius Ovidius Nāsō or Ovid to most of us,

00:01:04.672 --> 00:01:09.269
a Roman poet and the author of Metamorphoses,

00:01:09.269 --> 00:01:18.064
one of the great pieces of western literature that has been translated hundreds of times since its creation in the 8th century.

00:01:18.064 --> 00:01:28.888
Metamorphoses which means Books of Transformations details the history of the world from its creation all the way until the death of Julius Caesar

00:01:28.888 --> 00:01:36.567
and in between there are around 250 myths, based on the theme of Metamorphosis being transformed into something else.

00:01:36.567 --> 00:01:43.167
Many of these myths are very similar to their greek counterparts and others have slight changes.

00:01:43.167 --> 00:01:52.077
Today we will be taking a look at 5 stories, some of which are fairly well known and others less so.

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:04.089
The first story is the tragic tale of callisto.

00:02:04.089 --> 00:02:16.476
As with all these stories I will mention then Roman names of the deities but for the most part to make it easier for everyone including myself I’ll be referring to them by the Greek names.

00:02:16.476 --> 00:02:24.263
This story begins with Jove or Zeus scouring the lands surveying the affairs of men.

00:02:24.263 --> 00:02:32.182
In the woods his attention is caught by a beautiful huntress, Calisto the daughter of king Lycaon.

00:02:32.182 --> 00:02:42.051
Calisto is described as a warrior of Diana, which means she was a devout follower of Artemis or part of the cult of Artemis.

00:02:42.051 --> 00:02:49.683
The values of this cult were nature, hunting and chaste given Artemis was a virgin goddess.

00:02:49.683 --> 00:02:56.636
When Calisto found a place to rest in the forest she put down her bow and her spear,

00:02:56.636 --> 00:03:04.886
Assuming his wife Juno or Hera would never find out of this prank as Ovid refers to it Zeus appeared to Calisto.

00:03:04.886 --> 00:03:10.099
But not as himself, instead he took the form of Artemis,

00:03:10.099 --> 00:03:13.466
when Calisto saw her queen before her she elated,

00:03:13.466 --> 00:03:19.391
“Hail my Queen” “Greater than Jove I say though Jove should hear”

00:03:19.391 --> 00:03:27.847
Normally Zeus would take great offense to statement like this but in this case he was very pleased that he chose the right form to seduce Calisto.

00:03:27.847 --> 00:03:36.757
When Zeus made his advances his true appearance was revealed to Calisto and she tried to fend him off but was unsuccessful.

00:03:36.757 --> 00:03:46.723
Once he was done he retired to the heavens, leaving Calisto to wonder the forest distraught at having broken her vow of chastity.

00:03:46.723 --> 00:03:54.643
Zeus’ plan to have this affair disguised as Artemis was unsuccessful as Hera has watched the entire thing.

00:03:54.643 --> 00:04:04.097
Wandering through the forest Calisto came across Artemis once again, though this time she was hesitant, was this another of Zeus’s tricks.

00:04:04.097 --> 00:04:10.608
Followed by her nymphs and other followers Calisto recognised her patron Goddess,

00:04:10.608 --> 00:04:17.479
she was invited to bathe in one of Artemis’ secret springs known for their purity.

00:04:17.479 --> 00:04:25.828
When they reached the spring and nymphs and confirmed there were no prying eyes, they undressed and entered the water.

00:04:25.828 --> 00:04:32.366
The only one not to enter was Calisto who could no longer hide her look of shame,

00:04:32.366 --> 00:04:41.257
when she was stripped her secret was revealed. Gazing upon the shape of her body they could tell she was pregnant,

00:04:41.257 --> 00:04:47.381
“Begone!” Diana cried “You shall not stain My stream!”

00:04:47.381 --> 00:04:57.216
Calisto, pregnant with Zeus’ child was exiled from Artemis cult and everything she valued and worked for was taken from her.

00:04:57.216 --> 00:05:01.160
This was only the beginning of her punishment. Sadly,

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:09.593
Hera who had observed everything was waiting for the perfect time to take her revenge and that time was now.

00:05:09.593 --> 00:05:14.852
When she saw Calisto have given birth to a boy that was the final straw,

00:05:14.852 --> 00:05:23.362
she grabbed Calisto by the hair and flung her to the ground. Calisto begged for mercy but these prayers fell on deaf ears.

00:05:23.362 --> 00:05:36.167
She held out her arms and Hera laughed as those arms were covered in thick fur, he nails began to grow, the lips that Zeus lusted for became a hideous jaw.

00:05:36.167 --> 00:05:46.143
Her transformation into a bear was now complete, the only part of her that remained human was her heart so she could feel her pain and heartbreak.

00:05:46.143 --> 00:05:57.529
When she wandered through the woods it was now Calisto who was being hunted, she would experience the horror of the hunt as opposed to the thrill that she once so dearly enjoyed.

00:05:57.529 --> 00:06:06.098
Sixteen years later she came face to face with a young hunter she recognised, her son Arcas.

00:06:06.098 --> 00:06:11.557
When she called to him he saw nothing but a bear growling at him,

00:06:11.557 --> 00:06:18.789
Arcas took his javelin and just before he unknowingly killed his mother Zeus would intervene.

00:06:18.789 --> 00:06:24.591
He placed them both in the sky as constellations Ursa Major and Minor.

00:06:24.591 --> 00:06:31.852
Other versions end with Calisto being killed by Artemis or her own son.

00:06:31.852 --> 00:06:40.544
So although there isn’t too much to be happy about in this story at least it ends with mother and son being reunited.

00:06:42.114 --> 00:06:50.775
In the kingdom of Lydia the muses sung a beautiful song of a young weaver known as Arachne.

00:06:50.775 --> 00:06:58.884
Her mother had passed and her father Idmon of Colophon was known for his distinct purple dye.

00:06:58.884 --> 00:07:05.857
despite her humble upbringing Arachne’s work was known in all the surrounding kingdoms.

00:07:05.857 --> 00:07:15.185
The muses themselves would leave their home just to watch Arachne work, one would assume that she was trained by Pallas herself,

00:07:15.185 --> 00:07:19.483
but Arachne denied this claiming that her skill was her own.

00:07:19.483 --> 00:07:27.102
Pallas in this case refers to Athena and we know was the patron goddess of weaving and craft.

00:07:27.102 --> 00:07:33.485
This denial of Athena was enough hubris for her to pay Arachne a visit.

00:07:33.485 --> 00:07:40.542
“Let her contend with me. Should I lose, there’s no forfeit that I would not pay”

00:07:40.542 --> 00:07:48.103
Athena transformed herself into an old woman and travelled to meet this remarkable weaver.

00:07:48.103 --> 00:07:58.371
When she found her she gave  some advice, to be humble and ask for her harsh words against the gods to be pardoned.

00:07:58.371 --> 00:08:02.624
Arachne’s response both arrogant and provoking.

00:08:02.624 --> 00:08:27.171
“You’re too old, your brain has gone. You’ve lived too long, your years have done for you. Talk to your daughters, talk to your sons’ wives! Don’t think your words have any weight. My own advice is all I need. Why doesn’t Pallas come by herself? Why should she hesitate to match herself with me?”

00:08:27.171 --> 00:08:34.329
Having given Arachne the chance to apologise this response is the final straw for Athena.

00:08:34.329 --> 00:08:37.727
She threw away her disguise and reveals herself,

00:08:37.727 --> 00:08:43.187
the surrounding nymphs and women knelt for their patron goddess.

00:08:43.187 --> 00:08:48.357
And so they agreed to a contest to see whose skills were superior.

00:08:48.357 --> 00:08:52.887
Athena created a piece which showed the Olympians in all their glory.

00:08:52.887 --> 00:09:00.876
Arachne’s picture consisted of the same gods but she detailed what she believed to be their flaws.

00:09:00.876 --> 00:09:12.408
If Athena’s piece painted the gods in a positive light, Arachne’s was most certainly negative displaying the hubris that resulted in this contest to begin with.

00:09:12.408 --> 00:09:23.625
Inspecting Arachne’s work Athena could find no fault, the tapestry had been woven to perfection, however it’s content was a crime against the heavens.

00:09:23.625 --> 00:09:31.725
She tore up the tapestry, took the wooden shuttle she had used during the contest and struck Arachne on the forehead,

00:09:31.725 --> 00:09:36.062
after the fourth strike Arachne could take the punishment no longer.

00:09:36.062 --> 00:09:41.766
She placed a noose around her neck, but as she hung Athena caught and raised her,

00:09:41.766 --> 00:09:56.614
“Live. Yes, live but hang, you wicked girl, and know you’ll rue the future too: that penalty your kin shall pay to all prosperity!”

00:09:56.614 --> 00:10:01.147
Athena sprinkled her with the drugs of Hecate.

00:10:01.147 --> 00:10:08.296
Her hair began to fall out, her nose and ears followed. Her head shrunk along with the rest of her body.

00:10:08.296 --> 00:10:17.161
Instead of legs there were eight long fingers by her sides. From her belly came a fine-spun thread.

00:10:17.161 --> 00:10:26.341
As a spider Arachne was still able to weave her web, forever pursuing the former skills she once possessed but always falling short.

00:10:26.341 --> 00:10:43.401
In some versions of this story Arachne is somewhat of a sympathetic character but in Ovid’s story she is unlikely at best and it’s very hard to argue her punishment wasn’t justified.

00:10:43.401 --> 00:10:49.169
Pygmalion was a king in Cyprus as well as a renowned sculptor.

00:10:49.169 --> 00:10:54.806
His experiences with women were negative enough for him to take a vow of celibacy.

00:10:54.806 --> 00:11:01.732
He still yearned for the companionship of married love but he distracted himself with his work.

00:11:01.732 --> 00:11:13.006
He worked on a sculpture made of white ivory in the shape of a woman he thought to be perfect and more beautiful than any woman he had seen before.

00:11:13.006 --> 00:11:19.193
The more time he spent around the sculpture the more fell in love with his creation.

00:11:19.193 --> 00:11:27.045
The sculpture was so life-like he eventually could no longer distinguish between what was real and what wasn’t.

00:11:27.045 --> 00:11:34.074
He would talk to it, caress sculpture and even dress her as if she was real.

00:11:34.074 --> 00:11:43.928
The festival of Venus or Aphrodite was a very big occasion in Cyprus and when it came Pygmalion saw an opportunity.

00:11:43.928 --> 00:11:48.595
He made several offerings to Aphrodite at her altar,

00:11:48.595 --> 00:11:56.219
afraid to discuss his secret out loud he silently wished for a bride just like his ivory woman.

00:11:56.219 --> 00:12:05.723
When he returned home his prayers were answered, the goddess of love had transformed his ivory statue into a real woman.

00:12:05.723 --> 00:12:12.650
Pygmalion finally had the love and companionship he craved as weird as his methods were.

00:12:12.650 --> 00:12:22.621
Pygmalion’s sculpture is never named by Ovid but in the years that followed this sculpture turned women is referred to as Galatea.

00:12:26.339 --> 00:12:31.331
From their tower the boy could only imagine what it was like to be outside,

00:12:31.331 --> 00:12:37.549
Daedalus felt a greater sorrow for his son whose childhood had been taken from him.

00:12:37.549 --> 00:12:49.180
He understood why Minos had imprisoned him for the role he played in aiding Theseus to slay the minotaur but poor Icarus did not deserve to share in his fathers punishment.

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:58.342
The tower was surrounded by water on all sides, “Minos may own all else; he does not own the air.”

00:12:58.342 --> 00:13:05.210
Daedalus gathered rows of feathers and arranged them smallest first followed by the larger ones.

00:13:05.210 --> 00:13:13.238
He used reeds to create a shape that would immediate a bird's wings and held everything together with wax.

00:13:13.238 --> 00:13:18.594
Daedalus had created two pairs of wings for Icarus and he to escape Minos’ tower. When Icarus realised what his father had created he was overjoyed,

00:13:18.594 --> 00:13:23.003
fitting the wings Daedalus instructed his son.

00:13:23.003 --> 00:13:33.730
“Take care” He said, “ To fly a middle course, lest if you sink too low the waves may weight your feathers, if too high, the heat may burn them.

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:41.229
Fly half way between the two and do not watch the stars. Set your course where I shall lead.”

00:13:41.229 --> 00:13:47.659
As he inspected Icarus’ wings one final time he feared the worst,

00:13:47.659 --> 00:13:53.282
he kissed his son and jumped from the tower leading the way.

00:13:53.282 --> 00:14:00.432
Icarus followed his father closely but eventually he was distracted by the stars and flew too close to the sun.

00:14:00.432 --> 00:14:05.515
The wax started to melt and the wings begin to fall apart,

00:14:05.515 --> 00:14:11.330
Daedalus could only watch helplessly as his son plummeted to his death.

00:14:11.330 --> 00:14:19.696
He buried his son on a nearby island and that portion of the sea would forever bear Icarus’ name.

00:14:23.079 --> 00:14:32.455
Our last tale goes by the name of Mercury and Battus, the story of the theft of Apollo's cattle by Hermes.

00:14:32.455 --> 00:14:38.234
The story begins with Apollo in his field relaxing and playing music.

00:14:38.234 --> 00:14:46.528
Noticing him not paying attention Hermes snuck into the meadow and stole all of the cattle hiding them in the forest.

00:14:46.528 --> 00:14:52.125
There was only one witness to this crime, an elderly man know as Battus.

00:14:52.125 --> 00:15:05.855
Hermes approached the man “My good friend, whoever you are, If anyone enquires about this herd, say you’ve not seen them; and to thank you for that service take a cow for your reward”

00:15:05.855 --> 00:15:10.873
and so Hermes gave the man a cow,

00:15:10.873 --> 00:15:14.686
the man replied saying. that stone will tell sooner than I” pointing at a stone in the distance.

00:15:14.686 --> 00:15:21.736
And so having successfully bribed the man Hermes disappeared into the woods.

00:15:21.736 --> 00:15:27.277
Hermes however was no fool, he returned to test the old man's word.

00:15:27.277 --> 00:15:31.556
He appeared with a completely different voice and build,

00:15:31.556 --> 00:15:43.942
“Good fellow, help me; if you’ve seen some cattle hereabouts, speak up, they’re stolen; and you shall have a cow and a bull, a pair”

00:15:43.942 --> 00:15:49.817
This was double the reward Hermes offered and as predicted the old man replied.

00:15:49.817 --> 00:15:57.367
“There on yon hill they’ll be” the old man pointed to exactly where Hermes had taken the cattle.

00:15:57.367 --> 00:16:07.145
Hermes nodded and laughed, “You rogue, so you betray me to myself, me to myself, I say”

00:16:07.145 --> 00:16:16.546
As the man previously stated that stone would sooner tell his secret, Hermes saw the humour in transforming the man into a stone.

00:16:16.546 --> 00:16:25.816
A stone that would be called Tell-Tale. A reminder for all those to see, the consequence of treachery.

00:16:25.816 --> 00:16:38.226
That concludes our look at some of the stories from Ovid’s metamorphoses, If you’d like to see a second volume with 5 more stories then let me know your thoughts.

